  "query_preview": "..\n\nBenign conditions carry through the afternoon and evening as \nstability holds within the warmer return flow of high pressure now \nexiting off the Mid-Atlantic coast. Smoke models and AQI \nobservations indicate the remnant wildfire smoke will linger across \nthe region, but EGLE forecasts call for concentrations to gradually \ndecline so the Air Quality Alert will be allowed to expire tonight. \n\nA favorable setup for severe weather is in place over Wisconsin and \nthe western UP this afternoon and evening as a potent shortwave \npivots across the northern Great Lakes. Potential for supercells \nexists west of Lake Michigan with anticipated evolution into a \nbroken line or clusters of storms that migrate E to ESE into the \nlower peninsula late this evening. The best synoptic/upper jet \nforcing driving this convection is slated to pass overhead between \n02z and 10z tonight, outpacing the narrow plume of upstream \ninstability that will lag behind. Remnant low-level stability noted \nin model soundings and the nocturnal timing devoid of surface-based \ninstability likely result in a weakening trend as convection arrives \nlate this evening, and a transition of surviving convection to an \nelevated character. Peak coverage is anticipated between midnight \nand 6am, with scattered to numerous showers and embedded \nthunderstorms. The main threat would be isolated wind gusts in \nexcess of 40 mph, but this carries low confidence given the near-\nsurface stability. If upstream convection holds together and \nmarginal instability works into the western Saginaw Valley late this \nevening, this would be the most likely area to see severe wind \ngusts. A Marginal Risk for severe weather and excessive rainfall \nexists across the area tonight.\n\nA pocket of forced ascent along the LLJ passes over the southern \nforecast area between 12z and 15z Tuesday morning which offers \npotential for a flare-up of showers/storms, but latest CAM output \nsuggests the higher potential for convective response may ride the \nCAPE gradient and track farther south into IN/OH. Otherwise, \nincreasing low-level moisture and steepening mid-level lapse rates \ncause a plume of instability to spread across Lower MI Tuesday \nmorning ahead of the cold front set to track through in the late \nafternoon. This is sufficient to maintain a chance of scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms, especially across the Thumb. Will monitor \nareas east of I-75 for isolated strong to severe storms in the early \nafternoon while instability and shear briefly reach a peak. Breezy \nconditions amidst cold and dry advection ensue behind the front with \n850mb temps falling to around 5C Wednesday morning, translating to \nlows in the mid 50s.\n\nCool and dry conditions prevail Wednesday and Thursday as high \npressure eases across the region, with high temps in the 70s and \nlows in the 50s. Dry conditions persist into Friday with an uptick \nin temperatures closer to the seasonal average as southerly return \nflow ensues. The next window for more active weather looks to arrive \nlate in the weekend as the next cold front sweeps across the Great \nLakes.",
